---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import StorySteps from '../../components/StorySteps.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/charts/Figure.astro';
import Funnel from '../../components/charts/Funnel.astro';
import BarChart from '../../components/charts/BarChart.astro';
import StatBand from '../../components/charts/StatBand.astro';

const steps = [
  {
    kicker: 'The problem',
    headline: 'A CRM that recorded activity, not outcomes',
    body:
      'Leads arrived from half a dozen places and were worked in half a dozen ways. Custom fields meant different things to different people, pipeline stages were skipped, and outcomes often were not recorded at all — so the CRM could tell you what happened to a contact but not what any of it was worth.',
  },
  {
    kicker: 'What I built',
    headline: 'The CRM as the single source of truth',
    body:
      'A full architecture rebuild in GoHighLevel: a normalised custom-field schema, one canonical pipeline with enforced stage progression, calendars wired to the right teams, automations covering the whole lead lifecycle, and every acquisition platform integrated so a lead arrives already attributed.',
  },
  {
    kicker: 'The result',
    headline: 'Everything downstream became possible',
    body:
      'This is the unglamorous project that made the visible ones work. Reliable outcome capture is what let show rate be measured and then lifted, and consistent source attribution is what let the intelligence platform report on channel performance at all.',
  },
];

const integrations = [
  { name: 'Meta Ads', role: 'Lead-gen forms and campaign attribution flow in via the Graph API, so paid leads arrive tagged with campaign, ad set and creative.' },
  { name: 'GA4', role: 'Session and behaviour data joined on the contact record, connecting on-site behaviour to what happened after the form submit.' },
  { name: 'Google Search Console', role: 'Query and ranking data feeding the organic side of attribution and content prioritisation.' },
  { name: 'Website & chatbot', role: 'All web enquiry paths land in one intake, normalised to the same schema as every other source.' },
  { name: 'Calendars', role: 'Counsellor availability per team and location, so booking and outcome events are captured against the right owner.' },
  { name: 'Streamlit platform', role: 'The CRM is the upstream source for the reporting layer — one schema in, one set of numbers out.' },
];

const workflows = [
  { stage: 'Intake', title: 'Capture & normalise', desc: 'Every enquiry routed to one intake schema, deduplicated, and stamped with its true source before anything else happens.' },
  { stage: 'Routing', title: 'Assign & notify', desc: 'Round-robin assignment by team, location and availability, with the owning counsellor notified immediately.' },
  { stage: 'Nurture', title: 'Sequence & remind', desc: 'Lifecycle sequences by lead type, including the booking reminders and confirmation step that lifted show rate.' },
  { stage: 'Outcome', title: 'Record & reconcile', desc: 'Consultation outcomes forced to be recorded at close, then reconciled against payments so revenue ties back to source.' },
];

const pipeline = [
  { label: 'New lead', value: 1232 },
  { label: 'Booked', value: 431 },
  { label: 'Attended', value: 310 },
  { label: 'Initial requirements', value: 198 },
  { label: 'Paid', value: 97 },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="GoHighLevel CRM Architecture"
  eyebrow="Case study · CRM → Data governance"
  hook="Workflows, pipelines, calendars and platform integrations — rebuilt so that the CRM stopped being a contact list and started being the single source of truth."
  description="Full GoHighLevel build-out: normalised schema, enforced pipeline, lifecycle automations and native platform integrations establishing an enterprise single source of truth."
  org="The Migration"
  year="2026"
  stack={['GoHighLevel', 'Workflow Automation', 'Pipelines', 'Calendars', 'API Integration', 'SSOT Design', 'Data Governance']}
>
  <p class="lead">
    Nobody puts "cleaned up the custom fields" on a highlight reel. But every number in
    the other three case studies exists because this work happened first.
  </p>

  <StorySteps steps={steps} />

  <StatBand
    stats={[
      { value: '6', label: 'Platforms integrated into one record' },
      { value: '1', label: 'Canonical pipeline, enforced' },
      { value: '100%', label: 'Consultations with a recorded outcome' },
      { value: '12', label: 'Person marketing team on one schema' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Platforms integrated</h2>
  <p>
    The goal of every integration was the same: a lead should arrive already knowing
    where it came from, so that attribution is a property of the record rather than a
    reconciliation job someone does later.
  </p>

  <div class="int-grid">
    {integrations.map((i) => (
      <div class="card">
        <h3>{i.name}</h3>
        <p>{i.role}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>The lifecycle automations</h2>
  <p>
    Four workflow groups cover a lead from first touch to reconciled revenue. Each one
    exists to remove a place where data used to go missing.
  </p>

  <div class="flow viz">
    {workflows.map((w, i) => (
      <>
        <div class="flow-node">
          <span class="n-stage">{w.stage}</span>
          <span class="n-title">{w.title}</span>
          <span class="n-desc">{w.desc}</span>
        </div>
        {i < workflows.length - 1 && <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>}
      </>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>One pipeline, enforced</h2>
  <p>
    The single most valuable governance decision was collapsing several ad-hoc pipelines
    into one canonical set of stages that cannot be skipped. Stage progression became
    trustworthy, which is the precondition for every drop-off number the business now
    reports.
  </p>

  <Figure
    title="Canonical pipeline stages"
    sub="Each stage as a share of the one before it"
    note="Demo counts illustrating the stage structure."
  >
    <Funnel stages={pipeline} />
  </Figure>

  <p>
    Before this, "Attended" and "Initial requirements" were often the same click, so the
    stage where the most qualified leads were actually being lost did not exist as a
    measurable step at all.
  </p>

  <h2>What governance actually bought</h2>
  <p>
    Data governance sounds like overhead until you price what it unlocks. Three concrete
    examples from this build:
  </p>

  <Figure
    title="Record completeness, before and after the rebuild"
    sub="Share of contact records with the field populated correctly"
    note="Illustrative figures showing the direction and scale of the change."
  >
    <BarChart
      rows={[
        { label: 'Source attributed', value: 96, display: '96%' },
        { label: 'Outcome recorded', value: 100, display: '100%' },
        { label: 'Owner assigned', value: 99, display: '99%' },
        { label: 'Stage progression valid', value: 94, display: '94%' },
      ]}
      max={100}
      highlight={['Outcome recorded']}
      categoryHeader="Field"
      valueHeader="Complete"
    />
  </Figure>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Show rate became measurable.</strong> Forcing outcome capture at close is
      the entire reason the
      <a href="/projects/show-rate-recovery/">68% → 82% story</a> could be told — you
      cannot lift a number you cannot see.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Attribution became a property, not a project.</strong> Because source is
      stamped at intake, the
      <a href="/projects/marketing-intelligence-platform/">intelligence platform</a>
      reports channel performance without any manual reconciliation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The model had honest features.</strong> A clean schema is what made it
      possible to audit
      <a href="/projects/lead-conversion-model/">which fields were leaking</a> — you can
      only ask "when does this get written?" if the answer is consistent.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    That is the argument for doing this work first. It produces no chart of its own, and
    then it produces every other chart.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .int-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 15rem), 1fr));
    margin-block: 1.8rem 2.5rem;
  }
  .int-grid h3 { font-size: 1rem; }
  .int-grid p { font-size: .89rem; }
</style>
